<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    The knife a surgeon is holding (RimWorld: Damages_Misc's SurgicalCut). Its own file, not an addition to
    Damages.xml, per CLAUDE.md.

    Why it exists at all: Recipe_Surgery.OnSurgeryFailed used to hurt - and sometimes kill - the patient with
    plain Cut, the same DamageDef a raider's knife deals. Nothing could tell the two apart, because nothing
    read externalViolence; now that something does, an operation that goes wrong would have read as a murder
    committed on the operating table. It would have charged the storyteller's adaptation as a combat
    casualty, left everyone in the room a witnessed-death memory, and told the player their surgeon's patient
    "has been cut to death".

    externalViolence=false is the whole point of the def. Everything else is deliberately identical to Cut -
    same injury hediff, same Sharp armor category - so that the wound a botched operation leaves is exactly
    the wound it left before and only its classification has changed. (RimWorld gives SurgicalCut its own
    injury hediff too; this port has no separate surgical-cut injury, and inventing one would change what the
    wound does rather than what it means.)
  -->

  <DamageDef>
    <defName>SurgicalCut</defName>
    <label>surgical cut</label>
    <hediff>Cut</hediff>
    <hediffSolid>Crack</hediffSolid>
    <armorCategory>Sharp</armorCategory>
    <externalViolence>false</externalViolence>
    <deathMessage>{0} has died during surgery.</deathMessage>
  </DamageDef>

</Defs>
